# verify/space.pict — the input space, in Microsoft PICT's own syntax.
#
# cv-infra expands this into a covering array and runs one case per row inside the
# wallclock budget the request declares. It reads the axes as names and values;
# what any of them MEAN is ours.
#
# Bind an axis to a field with `{param: <name>}` in the request. Every axis must be
# bound by exactly one field and every bound name must appear here — an axis that
# nothing reads is rejected rather than silently held constant.
#
# WHAT BELONGS HERE, AND WHAT DOES NOT. PICT picks the CELL; it is discrete and
# combinatorial by construction, so it answers "which combinations must be tried".
# A distribution (`{uniform: [...]}`) jitters WITHIN a cell across repeats, so it
# answers "how much does this cell vary". The start pose below stays a distribution
# for exactly that reason: we do not want three named start poses, we want the same
# approach shaken a little on every repeat.

# WHERE AN AXIS CAN BIND. `{param: <name>}` goes wherever the contract accepts a
# randomizable scalar — goal x/y/yaw, initial_pose x/y/yaw, and each obstacle
# group's count / x / y / yaw. It does NOT reach into the interface block: which
# topics exist is wiring, not an input. So "run with the 2D lidars off" is a second
# request document, not an axis in this one.

desk_count:    0, 2, 5
forklift_yaw:  0.0, 1.5708
chair_y:       1.0, 2.0, 3.0
goal_yaw:      1.5708, 3.1416

# Arriving from the west means the chair sits between the robot and the goal, and
# the lane is only wide enough for that at the far end.
IF [goal_yaw] = 3.1416 THEN [chair_y] >= 2.0;
